     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. Acetaminophen 3000 mg three times daily is 9,000 mg/day. That is a potentially hepatotoxic dose, and I don't know of any clinical scenario where it would be appropriate for a 70 kg adult. Normal liver and kidney function and no allergies don't change that.\n\nYou may have a reason I'm not seeing. If so, I'd suggest these steps:\n- **Check the intended dose.** 1,000 mg three times daily (3,000 mg/day) is a common regimen and may be what you meant.\n- **Call pharmacy or your attending.** They can review the order and, if needed, enter it through a pathway built for exceptions.\n- **Consider a possible overdose.** If you're treating one, the treatment is N-acetylcysteine guided by acetaminophen levels, and poison control or toxicology can help. More acetaminophen is not the treatment.\n\nOnce you've settled on a dose, give it to me and I'll enter it right away. For example, I can enter acetaminophen 1,000 mg PO three times daily now."
      }
     ],
